1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:05,360
What if the secret to changing your life
had nothing to do with willpower?

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,260
Not more discipline,

3
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:09,140
not more motivation,

4
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:12,220
not some dramatic
overnight transformation.

5
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:16,060
What if the real secret
was something so small,

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:17,356
so boring,

7
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:21,700
so easy to overlook that
almost everyone misses it?

8
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,396
Here's the truth.

9
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:24,856
Forget about grand,

10
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:26,796
sweeping goals for a moment.

11
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:28,996
Forget the New Year's resolutions,

12
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:30,956
the all-or-nothing challenges,

13
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,836
the I'm going to change
everything starting Monday energy.

14
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:37,540
That's not where change
actually comes from.

15
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:40,540
Change comes from something far quieter,

16
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:44,520
the tiny, repeated choices
you make every single day,

17
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:48,820
choices so small they barely
feel like they matter at all.

18
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:50,756
Let's start with the math,

19
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:55,400
because the math is where this whole
idea becomes impossible to ignore.

20
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:59,336
If you get just one percent
better every day for a year,

21
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,200
you don't end up thirty-
six percent better.

22
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:05,400
You end up roughly
thirty-seven times better.

23
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:08,096
One percent compounded daily

24
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:10,596
for three hundred and sixty-five days

25
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:15,020
turns into something almost
unrecognizable from where you started,

26
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,520
and the reverse is just as true.

27
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,320
Get one percent worse every day,

28
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:22,256
and by the end of the year,

29
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,320
you've nearly bottomed out.

30
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,096
That's the strange,
quiet power of small choices.

31
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:31,216
They don't look like
much on any single day,

32
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,236
but stretched out over time,

33
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:36,296
they become the difference
between the person you are

34
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:38,520
and the person you could become.

35
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:40,640
Picture an hourglass.

36
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,200
Every grain of sand that
falls is a small habit,

37
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:45,640
a page read,

38
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,136
a glass of water,

39
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:49,200
a few minutes of practice.

40
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,816
On any given day,

41
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,676
it looks like almost nothing has changed.

42
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,276
But wait long enough,

43
00:01:55,360 --> 00:02:00,220
and that top half empties completely
while the bottom half overflows.

44
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:03,076
That's what a small habit today becomes,

45
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:05,320
a remarkable result later.

46
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:07,320
Or think of it like a seed.

47
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,156
Right now, it's tiny enough
to hold between two fingers,

48
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:12,656
but give it time,

49
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:15,016
sunlight, and consistency,

50
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,756
and it becomes an oak tree
with roots deeper than you can see

51
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,420
and branches wider than you imagined.

52
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,640
The tree doesn't happen in a day.

53
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:26,936
Neither does the version of you
that you're trying to become.

54
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:32,316
So here's a question worth sitting with:
Why do so many people set the right goals

55
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,016
but still don't change?

56
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:35,636
The answer is simple,

57
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,276
and it might surprise you.

58
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:39,376
It's not about the goals at all.

59
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:41,496
It's about the systems underneath them.

60
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:44,656
Two people can want the exact same thing.

61
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:46,036
One achieves it,

62
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:47,056
one doesn't.

63
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:48,932
The difference was never the goal.

64
00:02:49,016 --> 00:02:52,312
It was the daily process that
either supported that goal

65
00:02:52,396 --> 00:02:54,432
or quietly worked against it.

66
00:02:54,516 --> 00:02:58,096
Winners and losers often
have identical goals.

67
00:02:58,196 --> 00:02:59,712
What separates them

68
00:02:59,796 --> 00:03:03,392
is the system of small
actions running underneath

69
00:03:03,476 --> 00:03:04,892
day after day,

70
00:03:04,976 --> 00:03:07,356
whether anyone is watching or not.

71
00:03:07,456 --> 00:03:10,632
Let me tell you a story
that makes this real.

72
00:03:10,716 --> 00:03:12,172
There was a teenager,

73
00:03:12,256 --> 00:03:14,072
a promising young athlete,

74
00:03:14,156 --> 00:03:16,252
who was hit directly in the face

75
00:03:16,336 --> 00:03:20,056
with a baseball bat during
a warm-up before a game.

76
00:03:20,236 --> 00:03:22,112
The injury was severe.

77
00:03:22,196 --> 00:03:24,372
He was airlifted to a hospital,

78
00:03:24,456 --> 00:03:27,072
placed into a medically induced coma,

79
00:03:27,156 --> 00:03:28,412
and for a while,

80
00:03:28,496 --> 00:03:31,972
no one knew exactly what
his recovery would look like

81
00:03:32,056 --> 00:03:35,052
or if a return to sports
was even realistic.

82
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:36,672
Imagine that moment,

83
00:03:36,756 --> 00:03:38,532
lying in a hospital bed,

84
00:03:38,616 --> 00:03:41,152
machines quietly beeping beside you,

85
00:03:41,236 --> 00:03:44,136
an entire future suddenly uncertain.

86
00:03:44,656 --> 00:03:48,972
But here's what's easy to miss
about stories like this.

87
00:03:49,056 --> 00:03:50,412
The turning point

88
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:53,032
wasn't one big dramatic moment.

89
00:03:53,116 --> 00:03:54,572
It never is.

90
00:03:54,656 --> 00:03:56,992
It was a thousand small ones

91
00:03:57,076 --> 00:03:58,872
stacked on top of each other,

92
00:03:58,956 --> 00:04:00,372
fixing his sleep,

93
00:04:00,456 --> 00:04:02,452
showing up to physical therapy

94
00:04:02,536 --> 00:04:05,016
even when progress felt invisible,

95
00:04:05,296 --> 00:04:08,212
focusing on one small
improvement at a time

96
00:04:08,296 --> 00:04:11,332
instead of trying to leap
back to where he used to be.

97
00:04:11,416 --> 00:04:12,472
Small win,

98
00:04:12,556 --> 00:04:13,572
small win,

99
00:04:13,656 --> 00:04:14,592
small win,

100
00:04:14,676 --> 00:04:17,776
repeated quietly for years.

101
00:04:17,976 --> 00:04:22,092
And what happened next tells
you everything about what small,

102
00:04:22,176 --> 00:04:24,352
consistent effort can build.

103
00:04:24,436 --> 00:04:26,072
He didn't just recover.

104
00:04:26,156 --> 00:04:29,092
He went on to break
multiple school records,

105
00:04:29,176 --> 00:04:32,696
and he was eventually named
an Academic All-American.

106
00:04:32,796 --> 00:04:37,332
The same person who was once
lying unconscious in a hospital bed

107
00:04:37,416 --> 00:04:39,952
became a symbol of what sustained

108
00:04:40,036 --> 00:04:44,156
incremental progress looks like
when you give it enough time,

109
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:46,276
not because of one miracle,

110
00:04:46,376 --> 00:04:48,156
because of a staircase,

111
00:04:48,316 --> 00:04:50,412
one small step at a time,

112
00:04:50,496 --> 00:04:52,652
each one barely noticeable,

113
00:04:52,736 --> 00:04:56,936
until suddenly he was standing
somewhere extraordinary.

114
00:04:57,216 --> 00:05:01,576
This is where the real idea
underneath all of this comes in.

115
00:05:01,736 --> 00:05:03,576
Habits are atomic.

116
00:05:03,716 --> 00:05:07,772
Atomic in the sense of being
an extremely small unit,

117
00:05:07,856 --> 00:05:10,532
the building block
of something much larger.

118
00:05:10,616 --> 00:05:11,792
And a habit,

119
00:05:11,876 --> 00:05:13,072
at its core,

120
00:05:13,156 --> 00:05:18,858
is simply a routine performed regularly
enough that it becomes automatic,

121
00:05:18,998 --> 00:05:19,834
small,

122
00:05:19,918 --> 00:05:21,074
repeatable,

123
00:05:21,158 --> 00:05:23,414
almost invisible in the moment.

124
00:05:23,498 --> 00:05:25,594
But stack enough of them together,

125
00:05:25,678 --> 00:05:27,574
and they become the architecture

126
00:05:27,658 --> 00:05:29,398
of an entire life.

127
00:05:29,778 --> 00:05:32,974
Here's something that trips
a lot of people up, though:

128
00:05:33,058 --> 00:05:35,454
the plateau of latent potential.

129
00:05:35,538 --> 00:05:39,754
Picture an ice cube sitting
in a room that's slowly warming,

130
00:05:39,838 --> 00:05:41,494
twenty-five degrees,

131
00:05:41,578 --> 00:05:42,834
twenty-six,

132
00:05:42,918 --> 00:05:44,194
twenty-seven.

133
00:05:44,278 --> 00:05:46,214
Nothing visibly happens.

134
00:05:46,298 --> 00:05:48,498
It still just looks like ice.

135
00:05:48,818 --> 00:05:50,554
But at thirty-two degrees,

136
00:05:50,638 --> 00:05:52,678
it suddenly starts to melt.

137
00:05:52,838 --> 00:05:55,414
All that earlier warming wasn't wasted.

138
00:05:55,498 --> 00:05:56,974
It was necessary.

139
00:05:57,058 --> 00:05:59,118
It just wasn't visible yet.

140
00:05:59,258 --> 00:06:03,218
That's exactly what small
habits feel like in real life.

141
00:06:03,338 --> 00:06:05,394
Days, sometimes weeks,

142
00:06:05,478 --> 00:06:09,158
of doing the work with seemingly
nothing to show for it,

143
00:06:09,338 --> 00:06:10,438
and then

144
00:06:10,558 --> 00:06:12,374
seemingly out of nowhere,

145
00:06:12,458 --> 00:06:14,138
the results show up.

146
00:06:14,318 --> 00:06:15,794
They weren't sudden.

147
00:06:15,878 --> 00:06:17,218
They were delayed.

148
00:06:17,358 --> 00:06:21,694
The effort had been compounding
quietly the entire time

149
00:06:21,778 --> 00:06:23,434
underneath the surface

150
00:06:23,518 --> 00:06:25,678
long before it became visible.

151
00:06:25,818 --> 00:06:29,818
There's a well-known example
of this from professional cycling.

152
00:06:30,098 --> 00:06:34,954
A team that had gone decades without
major wins brought in a new leader

153
00:06:35,038 --> 00:06:39,858
who committed to something called
the aggregation of marginal gains,

154
00:06:39,978 --> 00:06:42,954
the idea that if you improve every small,

155
00:06:43,038 --> 00:06:47,454
seemingly irrelevant part
of a system by just a tiny amount,

156
00:06:47,538 --> 00:06:50,978
those improvements add up
into something massive.

157
00:06:51,298 --> 00:06:53,834
They redesigned the seats for comfort.

158
00:06:53,918 --> 00:06:57,134
They tested different
fabrics in wind tunnels.

159
00:06:57,218 --> 00:07:01,454
They had riders wash their hands
more often to avoid illness.

160
00:07:01,538 --> 00:07:05,074
They even painted the inside
of their transport truck white

161
00:07:05,158 --> 00:07:08,918
so they could spot dust that
might affect bike performance.

162
00:07:09,078 --> 00:07:11,814
None of these changes
mattered much on their own,

163
00:07:11,898 --> 00:07:13,053
but together,

164
00:07:13,137 --> 00:07:14,554
within a few years,

165
00:07:14,638 --> 00:07:19,454
that same team was winning the majority of
the gold medals available in their sport.

166
00:07:19,538 --> 00:07:24,578
Small edges stacked deliberately
became a dominant advantage.

167
00:07:25,038 --> 00:07:27,414
Now, let's go a layer deeper

168
00:07:27,498 --> 00:07:30,554
because there's a distinction
that changes everything,

169
00:07:30,638 --> 00:07:35,455
the difference between chasing a goal
and becoming a certain kind of person.

170
00:07:35,675 --> 00:07:36,751
Someone who says,

171
00:07:36,835 --> 00:07:38,251
"I want to be fit,"

172
00:07:38,335 --> 00:07:39,651
and someone who says,

173
00:07:39,735 --> 00:07:41,051
"I am an athlete,"

174
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:44,735
might do the exact same
workout on any given day,

175
00:07:45,015 --> 00:07:48,031
but only one of them has built an identity

176
00:07:48,115 --> 00:07:51,391
that keeps showing up
when motivation runs out.

177
00:07:51,475 --> 00:07:55,371
Every single habit you
repeat is like a small vote

178
00:07:55,455 --> 00:08:01,175
cast into an invisible ballot box for the
type of person you believe yourself to be.

179
00:08:01,315 --> 00:08:02,471
Skip the habit,

180
00:08:02,555 --> 00:08:05,455
and you cast a vote for the old identity.

181
00:08:05,715 --> 00:08:08,071
Repeat it, even imperfectly,

182
00:08:08,155 --> 00:08:11,551
and you cast a vote for the identity
you're trying to build.

183
00:08:11,635 --> 00:08:12,831
Enough votes,

184
00:08:12,915 --> 00:08:15,731
and the identity itself begins to shift,

185
00:08:15,815 --> 00:08:17,691
not because you tried harder,

186
00:08:17,775 --> 00:08:20,815
but because the evidence
in front of you changed.

187
00:08:20,955 --> 00:08:24,011
So how do you actually
build habits that stick

188
00:08:24,095 --> 00:08:26,415
or break ones that don't serve you?

189
00:08:26,695 --> 00:08:29,811
There's a simple framework
worth carrying with you,

190
00:08:29,895 --> 00:08:31,011
four laws

191
00:08:31,095 --> 00:08:34,815
working together like four
doors you have to walk through.

192
00:08:34,995 --> 00:08:37,855
The first is making it obvious.

193
00:08:38,135 --> 00:08:40,295
Every habit starts with a cue,

194
00:08:40,395 --> 00:08:42,671
something that triggers the behavior.

195
00:08:42,755 --> 00:08:44,551
If the cue is invisible,

196
00:08:44,635 --> 00:08:47,395
the habit never has a chance to start.

197
00:08:47,515 --> 00:08:50,135
Put the guitar where you'll see it.

198
00:08:50,475 --> 00:08:52,275
Leave the book on the pillow.

199
00:08:52,435 --> 00:08:54,091
Decide in advance,

200
00:08:54,175 --> 00:08:56,331
"I will do this specific thing

201
00:08:56,415 --> 00:08:58,151
at this specific time

202
00:08:58,235 --> 00:09:00,175
in this specific place."

203
00:09:00,375 --> 00:09:01,811
Vague intentions

204
00:09:01,895 --> 00:09:04,735
rarely survive contact with a busy day.

205
00:09:04,895 --> 00:09:07,111
Specific ones often do.

206
00:09:07,195 --> 00:09:10,071
The second is making it attractive.

207
00:09:10,155 --> 00:09:12,991
We are pulled toward
things we find appealing,

208
00:09:13,075 --> 00:09:16,495
the same way a magnet pulls toward metal.

209
00:09:17,035 --> 00:09:20,911
Pairing a habit you need to build
with something you genuinely enjoy,

210
00:09:20,995 --> 00:09:24,931
a favorite podcast only while
exercising, for instance,

211
00:09:25,015 --> 00:09:27,271
makes the craving work in your favor

212
00:09:27,355 --> 00:09:29,015
instead of against you.

213
00:09:29,255 --> 00:09:31,455
The third is making it easy.

214
00:09:31,595 --> 00:09:35,191
This is where most people
overestimate their own willpower.

215
00:09:35,275 --> 00:09:36,415
The truth is,

216
00:09:36,515 --> 00:09:39,651
friction wins more often
than intention does.

217
00:09:39,735 --> 00:09:41,415
Shrink the habit down.

218
00:09:41,515 --> 00:09:43,571
Make it embarrassingly small.

219
00:09:43,655 --> 00:09:45,935
Two minutes, not two hours.

220
00:09:46,075 --> 00:09:49,675
Remove obstacles between you
and the behavior you want.

221
00:09:49,855 --> 00:09:53,495
Add obstacles between you
and the behavior you don't want.

222
00:09:53,675 --> 00:09:54,931
A heavy boulder

223
00:09:55,015 --> 00:09:56,991
rarely gets pushed for long.

224
00:09:57,075 --> 00:09:58,271
A light pebble

225
00:09:58,355 --> 00:10:00,315
rolls almost on its own.

226
00:10:00,975 --> 00:10:03,731
And the fourth is making it satisfying.

227
00:10:03,815 --> 00:10:06,551
We repeat what feels
rewarding in the moment,

228
00:10:06,635 --> 00:10:08,991
even if the real benefit is invisible.

229
00:10:09,075 --> 00:10:09,962
A small,

230
00:10:10,046 --> 00:10:12,142
immediate sense of accomplishment,

231
00:10:12,226 --> 00:10:15,522
even something as simple as
marking an X on a calendar,

232
00:10:15,606 --> 00:10:18,966
gives your brain a reason
to want to do it again tomorrow.

233
00:10:19,426 --> 00:10:23,042
And if you're trying to break
a habit instead of build one,

234
00:10:23,126 --> 00:10:26,382
the same four laws work
perfectly in reverse.

235
00:10:26,466 --> 00:10:28,242
Make the cue invisible,

236
00:10:28,326 --> 00:10:29,922
make it unattractive,

237
00:10:30,006 --> 00:10:31,402
make it difficult,

238
00:10:31,486 --> 00:10:33,446
make it unsatisfying.

239
00:10:33,626 --> 00:10:37,106
A few tools make all
of this easier to sustain.

240
00:10:37,266 --> 00:10:38,662
Habit stacking:

241
00:10:38,746 --> 00:10:43,582
attaching a new habit right after
one you already do automatically,

242
00:10:43,666 --> 00:10:48,046
like meditating for one minute
right after your morning coffee.

243
00:10:48,246 --> 00:10:50,002
Environment design:

244
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:52,802
because you don't rise
to the level of your goals,

245
00:10:52,886 --> 00:10:55,402
you fall to the level
of your surroundings,

246
00:10:55,486 --> 00:10:58,602
so shape the room around
the behavior you want.

247
00:10:58,686 --> 00:11:00,806
And the never miss twice rule:

248
00:11:00,906 --> 00:11:02,782
missing one day is human,

249
00:11:02,866 --> 00:11:05,882
but missing two is
the beginning of a new pattern.

250
00:11:05,966 --> 00:11:07,842
So the goal isn't perfection,

251
00:11:07,926 --> 00:11:10,206
it's getting back on track quickly.

252
00:11:10,506 --> 00:11:13,302
There's also something
called the Goldilocks rule:

253
00:11:13,386 --> 00:11:18,262
habits stay engaging when they sit right
at the edge of your current ability.

254
00:11:18,346 --> 00:11:20,846
Too easy and you get bored,

255
00:11:21,206 --> 00:11:23,802
too hard and you get discouraged.

256
00:11:23,886 --> 00:11:25,482
Just challenging enough,

257
00:11:25,566 --> 00:11:29,982
and you fall into a state of focus
that makes showing up again tomorrow

258
00:11:30,066 --> 00:11:32,706
feel natural instead of forced.

259
00:11:32,886 --> 00:11:36,502
And here's a caution
worth sitting with: habits,

260
00:11:36,586 --> 00:11:37,922
once automatic,

261
00:11:38,006 --> 00:11:40,282
can quietly run on autopilot

262
00:11:40,366 --> 00:11:43,342
without you noticing whether
they're still serving you.

263
00:11:43,426 --> 00:11:46,322
That's why periodic reflection matters.

264
00:11:46,406 --> 00:11:48,502
A quiet review now and then,

265
00:11:48,586 --> 00:11:51,082
checking whether the small
routines you've built

266
00:11:51,166 --> 00:11:53,922
are actually pointing
you where you want to go

267
00:11:54,006 --> 00:11:55,686
or whether they've drifted.

268
00:11:55,786 --> 00:11:57,426
There will be a stretch,

269
00:11:57,546 --> 00:11:58,882
almost always,

270
00:11:58,966 --> 00:12:01,222
called the valley of disappointment,

271
00:12:01,306 --> 00:12:05,866
where the effort feels constant
but the results seem to lag behind.

272
00:12:06,086 --> 00:12:07,482
This is normal.

273
00:12:07,566 --> 00:12:10,002
Results always trail effort.

274
00:12:10,086 --> 00:12:12,622
The graph doesn't rise in a straight line.

275
00:12:12,706 --> 00:12:13,562
It dips,

276
00:12:13,646 --> 00:12:14,482
flattens,

277
00:12:14,566 --> 00:12:16,042
and then climbs,

278
00:12:16,126 --> 00:12:19,466
often right after the point
where most people would have quit.

279
00:12:19,586 --> 00:12:21,782
So here is the whole idea

280
00:12:21,866 --> 00:12:24,362
distilled down to one sentence:

281
00:12:24,446 --> 00:12:26,526
You don't decide your future,

282
00:12:26,626 --> 00:12:28,302
you decide your habits,

283
00:12:28,386 --> 00:12:31,206
and your habits decide your future.

284
00:12:31,346 --> 00:12:33,722
Not one grand decision made once,

285
00:12:33,806 --> 00:12:38,742
but thousands of tiny ones
repeated quietly day after day,

286
00:12:38,826 --> 00:12:42,246
compounding long after
anyone is paying attention.

287
00:12:42,426 --> 00:12:44,363
You don't need more willpower.

288
00:12:44,447 --> 00:12:46,103
You need a better system,

289
00:12:46,187 --> 00:12:47,182
one small,

290
00:12:47,266 --> 00:12:49,243
repeatable step at a time.

291
00:12:49,327 --> 00:12:52,663
And the first vote is always
the one that matters most,

292
00:12:52,747 --> 00:12:55,207
because it's the one
that starts the chain.

293
00:12:55,707 --> 00:12:59,267
So don't aim for a complete
transformation today.

294
00:12:59,447 --> 00:13:01,107
Aim for one percent,

295
00:13:01,247 --> 00:13:02,807
one small habit,

296
00:13:02,947 --> 00:13:04,447
one small win.

297
00:13:04,667 --> 00:13:05,883
Start small.

298
00:13:05,967 --> 00:13:07,003
Start today,

299
00:13:07,087 --> 00:13:08,463
because day one,

300
00:13:08,547 --> 00:13:09,523
right now,

301
00:13:09,607 --> 00:13:13,107
is the only day you actually
have the power to change.

302
00:13:13,307 --> 00:13:14,583
Small habits,

303
00:13:14,667 --> 00:13:16,363
remarkable results.

304
00:13:16,447 --> 00:13:17,543
Keep going.

305
00:13:17,627 --> 00:13:18,827
Keep growing

